# Windows leg: the last 42 red tests, traced to five mechanisms and fixed at the root (2026-10-03)

Baseline for everything below is run `37126354066` (head `9e77c6d0`), job
`111218903002`, `platform tests (windows-latest)`. Both test steps of that job are
quoted as the runner printed them. That job had no other Windows failure, so 42
tests is the whole remainder of the Windows debt this batch deals with.

== 0. What the leg said before any of this

    $ cargo test (target-OS crates: except xai-grok-tools)   # --lib, per crate
        test result: FAILED. 67 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; ... finished in 13.28s
            local_terminal::tests::test_basic_command_output

    $ cargo test (target-OS crates: xai-grok-tools)          # --lib
        test result: FAILED. 3054 passed; 41 failed; ...
            37  implementations::lsp::tests
             3  implementations::grok_build::read_file::tests
             1  implementations::lsp::watched_files::tests

The 42 split into five mechanisms. Four of them were product or test-fixture
defects with an identifiable root cause; one (section 6) was the test's own budget.
None of them needed a Windows machine to fix, and every one of them is now covered
by a test that runs on Linux.

== 1. Cluster A, 37 tests: the mock LSP servers framed their answers in text mode

Every one of the 37 reported the same one-clause symptom:

    LSP initialization failed: '<name>' [<cmdline>] service stopped

`service stopped` is what async-lsp says when the stream ends without a parseable
message. The client's previous diagnostics work (commit `91085571` era, see
`docs/verification/platform-ci-2026-10-02.log` section 6) had already added the
server name, the command line, the exit code and the last stderr lines, and on
Windows all of that was useless: the exit code was 0 and the stderr tail was empty,
because the server had run correctly, answered, and been refused.

The mechanism is in the fixtures, not in the product. Each mock server wrote its
frames through Python's text-mode `sys.stdout`:

    print("Content-Length: %d\r\n\r\n%s" % (len(body), body))

On Windows, text mode rewrites every `\n` written to a pipe as `\r\n`. The header
terminator therefore left the interpreter as `\r\r\n\r\r\n`, which is not the
`\r\n\r\n` the protocol specifies. The parser refused it, the client stopped
reading and dropped its handle to the child's stdin, Python read EOF and exited 0.
Nothing in that chain raises an error, which is why 37 tests failed with a message
containing no facts.

The mechanism was confirmed on Linux by feeding the client the bytes Windows
produces. The durable form of that fixture is
`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/tests/mock_servers.rs::write_translated_newline_server`,
which overrides the framing helper with

    chunk.replace(b'\n', b'\r\n')

and the assertion is
`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/tests.rs::a_server_whose_bytes_are_wrong_shows_the_bytes`.
Run against the pre-fix client it failed with exactly the Windows sentence:

    LSP initialization failed: 'translated' [python3 -u .../translated_lsp.py]
    service stopped; the process exited with code 0

Fix: all five mock servers now frame in binary. Reads go through
`sys.stdin.buffer.readline()`, header keys are compared as bytes, and the write
path is

    def send_message(msg):
        body = json.dumps(msg).encode('utf-8')
        sys.stdout.buffer.write(b"Content-Length: %d\r\n\r\n" % len(body) + body)
        sys.stdout.buffer.flush()

The module doc of `tests/mock_servers.rs` states the rule and this failure, so the
next mock server written here does not rediscover it.

== 2. The interpreter probe: a name on PATH is not an interpreter

The fixtures pick an interpreter rather than assuming `python3`. Answering
`--version` with exit 0 is not sufficient evidence, because that is exactly what
the Microsoft Store execution alias does: it exists, it exits 0, it runs nothing.
A candidate is now an interpreter only if it executes a script, and the probe
requires a marker in the raw stdout of

    <candidate> [-3] -u -c "import sys; sys.stdout.write('chaos-lsp-probe-ok'); sys.stdout.flush()"

The three refusal clauses name the candidate and say which test it failed:
`cannot run a script` (could not spawn), `refused a script (<exit>, stderr: ...)`
(non-zero exit), `exited 0 on a script without running it (output: ...)`. The last
clause is the Store alias. The marker is matched against the untrimmed stdout, so a
stub that prints a banner first cannot slip past on a trimmed tail. Every refusal
is kept and printed when all candidates are rejected; `every_rejected_candidate_is_named_in_the_refusals`
asserts that two bad candidates produce two named refusals rather than one
aggregate message.

== 2b. Making those tests run on the platform whose alias they describe

The four tests above were `#[cfg(unix)]` at the time of the inventory in
`docs/verification/platform-gated-tests-2026-10-03.log`, and they had to be: a candidate that
"spawns, complains and leaves" was built as a shell script with a `#!` line and `chmod 755`, which
is not spawnable on Windows. So the test for a Windows-only failure mode was the one thing Windows
never ran. Two of those rows sat in the ledger as `assumptions=none` gates.

The stub is now built out of the interpreter the host has already proved works, which needs no
platform-specific file at all. Python consumes the first `-c` and treats everything after it as
`sys.argv`, so

    python -c "<stub body>" -u -c "<the probe's own script>"

runs the stub body and never reaches the script the probe handed it. That is the alias's shape:
spawns, prints, exits. `alias_args()` builds the candidate's argument list by taking the host
interpreter's own pre-args (`-3` where the launcher needs it) and appending the stub body, so the
test never hard-codes an interpreter name.

The second lesson cost a wasted mutation round. A refusal quotes the candidate's whole command
line, and the first version of these fixtures put the stub's message inside the `-c` body. The
message was therefore in the refusal whether or not the probe had read a single byte of output, and
`assert!(refusal.contains(ALIAS_NOTICE))` proved nothing about the code under test. Two mutations
survived for exactly that reason (section 8). The fixtures now carry the message in a file the stub
reads at run time (`stub_that_echoes()` / `message_file()`), and the file is deliberately named
`a.txt` so that no substring the assertions look for can be satisfied by the quoted path either.
After that change the same mutations are killed.

Four tests lost their `#[cfg(unix)]`; two of them were ledger rows, which is why the ledger is 1,106
rows instead of 1,108 and the assumption-free count is 441 instead of 443. Both budgets were
lowered to match, in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and in `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`, and the
lowering is checked in section 10 of the platform-gated log.

== 3. What a server actually sent: the startup trace

Even with correct fixtures, the next server to fail this way on any machine would
have produced the same useless report. `client.rs` now wraps both stdio streams of
a stdio server (`TraceReader` / `TraceWriter` in
`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/startup_trace.rs`) and, on a
startup failure only, appends what went out and what came back. The first 200 bytes
are kept, the rest is counted; recording stops the moment `initialize` is answered,
so a healthy long-lived server costs one relaxed atomic load per read and keeps
nothing. Control characters are spelled out, because a raw `\r` in a CI log is
invisible and `\r` is precisely the byte this exists to reveal.

The message the shipped code produces for the section 1 fixture, captured from a
run against the real client and real child process:

    LSP initialization failed: 'translated' [python3 -u /tmp/.tmpyiwGPa/translated_framing_lsp.py]
    service stopped; the process exited with code 0; 777 bytes were sent to it;
    it wrote 104 bytes on stdout: "Content-Length: 80\r\r\n\r\r\n{\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",
    \"id\": 0, \"result\": {\"capabilities\": {\"textDocumentSync\": 1}}}"

Before, the same event was `service stopped`. Now the doubled carriage return is
readable, and `777 bytes were sent to it` separately rules out the other hypothesis
(that the client never wrote the request).

== 4. Cluster D, 3 tests: the error message blamed a path the tool never used

    left:  "Error: C:/wrong/root/review/SKILL.md does not exist.\nNote: your current
            working directory is C:\\Users\\RUNNER~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\.tmp7fupvh..."
    right: "Error: /wrong/root/review/SKILL.md does not exist.\n..."

The tool resolves a model-supplied path with `resolve_model_path`
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/types/resources.rs`), which deliberately places
a rooted-but-not-absolute argument *inside* the working directory. The message that
announces where it looked was built with `PathBuf::join`, and on Windows `push`
"replaces everything except for the prefix (if any) of self" when the argument has
a root. `cwd.join("/wrong/root/x")` therefore yielded `C:/wrong/root/x`: an error
message naming a file that no lookup ever opened. `Path::is_absolute()` is false for
that argument on Windows and true on Unix, which is the entire reason no Linux run
could see this.

Fix: `crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/util/fs.rs::join_announced_path`, which
returns the argument verbatim when it is absolute here and otherwise appends it one
component at a time. Seven announcement sites in `read_file`, `search_replace` and
`grok_build_hashline::edit` now use it, so announcement and lookup agree by
construction instead of by coincidence.

The three fixtures were also wrong, in a way that hid the defect on Linux: they
passed `/wrong/root/...` and expected it to name a path outside the workspace, which
is precisely the claim Windows rejects. They now create a second `TempDir` and ask
for a file genuinely outside the announced working directory on every platform, so
the assertion tests the announcement rather than a platform accident.

Honest limit: on Linux `join_announced_path` and `push` return the same string for
every input. No Linux assertion can distinguish them, and this batch does not claim
otherwise. What is proven locally is that the fixtures observe the join at all
(mutation M7 in section 8), and that the component walk they depend on
(`join_relative`) keeps the base in front of a rooted argument.

== 5. Cluster E, 1 test: a glob with an unresolvable base was applied to everything

    implementations::lsp::tests::... panicked at watched_files.rs:447:9:
    assertion failed: !watched.watches(&root/App.dll, FileChangeType::CHANGED)

`GlobPattern::Relative` carries a `baseUri`. When the client could not turn that URI
into a local path, the code fell back to the workspace root, which silently widens
the server's pattern to the whole workspace. A real server sending `**/*.dll` with a
base this client cannot name would have had every `App.dll` in the project watched
and reported. On Windows the fallback was reachable from a server that had behaved
perfectly on Linux, because `file:///` URIs without a drive do not resolve there.

Fix: there is no fallback. An unresolvable base yields no base and `in_workspace` is
computed from the base the client actually has. The new test
`an_unresolvable_base_uri_is_never_treated_as_the_workspace` feeds
`"baseUri": "https://example.invalid/packages"` with pattern `**/*` and asserts both
that the registration is accepted and that nothing in the workspace is watched by
it; the sibling `out_of_workspace_globs_never_match` keeps the original Windows
assertion.

== 6. Cluster B, 1 test: the shell was fine, the test's budget was not

`local_terminal::tests::test_basic_command_output` printed

    assertion `left == right` failed
      left: ""
     right: "hello"

and that message hid the actual event. The `FAILED` line for this test is stamped
`14:30:45.913` while the previous completion in the same binary is stamped
`14:30:35.924`, and the binary as a whole reports `finished in 13.28s`. The gap is
the test's own 10-second request timeout, so the run had been killed and the pipes
closed with nothing read. `assert_eq!` reported the first of two assertions and
never got as far as `exit_code`, which was `None`, or `timed_out`, which was `true`.

The machine could run the command. In the same job, the same step, the sibling test
`streaming_local_terminal::tests::test_streaming_sends_status_updates` runs the same
`echo` through the streaming runner with a 30-second budget and passes, and it is
that test's duration that accounts for most of the 13.28s the binary reported. The
first shell of a Windows CI job pays for whatever the host does to an executable it
has never run; 10 seconds was less than that.

Fix, in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell-terminal/src/local_terminal.rs`:
the request budget matches the sibling (30s, with the measured numbers in the
comment), and a failure now prints the shell it reached, the elapsed time, the exit
code, `timed_out`, `truncated`, the byte count and the raw output. On Unix the
command timeout the bash tool actually hands down is 120s, so this bounds the test
and not the product. The underlying question of what the first PowerShell start on
a cold Windows host costs is not answered here and is not claimed to be.

== 7. Persistent shell: the request used to vanish (TODO row for M2.3)

`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/terminal.rs` gates its persistent
shell on `#[cfg(unix)]` inside the actor, so on Windows a caller that asked for one
got one shell per command with no error, no log, and no test able to say which of
the two behaviours it had. The row allowed either an explicit failure or at least
one `warn`; the gap is now both observable and testable off Unix:

    persistent_shell_supported()            -> the platform predicate, one place
    reconcile_persistent_shell(..)          -> cfg-free decision, reports through a
                                               caller-supplied closure at most once
                                               per latch
    LocalTerminalBackend::persistent_shell_effective()  -> what the caller can ask

The decision and its reporting contain no `cfg`; `supported` is the only platform
input, which is what lets the Windows path run in a Linux test. The warn fires once
per process however many sessions ask, and
`a_persistent_request_without_a_persistent_shell_is_refused_and_said_once` drives
three requests through it and asserts one report and a spent latch.
`the_backend_reports_the_shell_mode_it_actually_got` drives the real constructor,
so a backend cannot report a mode its actor does not have.

The Windows *implementation* of a persistent shell is still not done. This closes
the "silently ignored" part, not the "no persistent shell on Windows" part; the TODO
row stays open on that half.

== 8. Non-vacuity: 11 mutations against the shipped code and the fixtures

Each mutation is an exact-string edit to the production or fixture source, followed
by the real test binary, followed by a restore verified with `cmp`.

    M1  startup_trace: ignore the disarm flag
        recording_stops_when_startup_is_settled ... FAILED
    M2  startup_trace: count the bytes but keep none of them
        the_first_bytes_are_kept_and_the_rest_is_only_counted ... FAILED
        the_bytes_that_came_back_survive_into_the_message ... FAILED
    M3  startup_trace: keep every byte (drop the 200-byte cap)
        the_first_bytes_are_kept_and_the_rest_is_only_counted ... FAILED
    M4  startup_trace: write the preview without escaping
        the_bytes_that_came_back_survive_into_the_message ... FAILED
    M5  mock_servers: accept a candidate that exits 0 without running the script
        a_stub_that_answers_version_but_runs_nothing_is_not_an_interpreter ... FAILED
        every_rejected_candidate_is_named_in_the_refusals ... FAILED
    M6  watched_files: restore the workspace-root fallback
        an_unresolvable_base_uri_is_never_treated_as_the_workspace ... FAILED
    M7  fs: drop the is_absolute early return from join_announced_path
        missing_skill_read_uses_display_path ... FAILED
        missing_skill_read_suggests_registered_path ... FAILED
        missing_skill_read_omits_ambiguous_suggestion ... FAILED
        missing_skill_read_omits_stale_registered_path ... FAILED
    M8  terminal: hand out the persistent shell that was requested regardless of
        support (the old behaviour)
        a_persistent_request_without_a_persistent_shell_is_refused_and_said_once ... FAILED
    M9  mock_servers: stop trying candidates after the first refusal
        a_stub_that_refuses_to_run_is_not_an_interpreter ... FAILED
        every_rejected_candidate_is_named_in_the_refusals ... FAILED
    M10 mock_servers: drop what the candidate printed from the refusal
        a_stub_that_answers_version_but_runs_nothing_is_not_an_interpreter ... FAILED
        every_rejected_candidate_is_named_in_the_refusals ... FAILED
    M11 mock_servers: treat a non-zero exit as a fine answer
        every_rejected_candidate_is_named_in_the_refusals ... FAILED

M9, M10 and M11 target `probe_python` / `resolve_python`, which live in the test-support module
rather than in shipped product code. That is still the right thing to weaken here: that resolver is
what decided which interpreter 37 LSP tests ran under, and a regression in it reproduces the exact
blindness this batch is about. It is stated plainly so nobody reads these three as evidence about
shipped interpreter discovery, which does not exist.

M7 is the one that needed reading afterwards rather than trusting the driver: on
Linux it cannot fail the Windows-specific unit test, and it is the four rewritten
fixtures that go red, which is the evidence that they observe the join.

M10 and M11 are also the two mutations that initially survived, and the reason is the most
transferable thing in this batch. The refusal text quotes the candidate's command line, and the
stub's message had been written into the `-c` body of that command line, so the assertion
`refusal.contains(message)` was satisfied by the fixture's own spelling no matter what the probe
captured. `probe treats a non-zero exit as fine` survived for the related reason that a message
present in the refusal is not the same fact as a refusal being produced. Both fixtures now read the
message from a file at run time (section 2b), and under the same drivers each mutation exits 101
with the tests named above failing and `cmp` confirming the byte-identical restore:

    summary: probe accepts a candidate that runs nothing: rc=101; resolver gives up after the
    first refusal: rc=101; refusal drops what the candidate said: rc=101; probe treats a
    non-zero exit as fine: rc=101
    === restored tree: rc=0

One trap worth recording, because it produced a false result rather than a false
green. The driver restored each file with `shutil.copy2`, which puts the
*pre-mutation* mtime back. Cargo's fingerprint compares mtimes, so it called the
already-built mutated binary fresh and re-ran it. A restored tree was therefore
tested against mutated code, and one test correctly failed for the wrong reason.
The fix is to bump the mtime after restoring; the run in section 9 is from a tree
where that was done and the crate was rebuilt from source.

== 9. Local verification after the restore

Re-run once on the final tree, after the interpreter fixtures were rewritten
(section 2b) and after every mutated file had been restored and rebuilt:

    $ cargo fmt --check -p xai-grok-tools -p xai-grok-shell-terminal
        (no output)
    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib
        test result: ok. 3201 passed; 0 failed; 3 ignored; finished in 95.68s
    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-shell-terminal --lib
        test result: ok. 76 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; finished in 2.65s
    $ cargo clippy -p xai-grok-tools -p xai-grok-shell-terminal --all-targets -- -D warnings
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 1m 09s

Filters, all against the same build:

    interpreter_tests                    5 passed  / 0 failed / 0 ignored
    implementations::lsp::             125 passed  / 0 failed / 2 ignored
    util::fs::tests                     14 passed  / 0 failed / 0 ignored
    computer::local::terminal::tests    71 passed  / 0 failed / 0 ignored
    read_file::tests                   120 passed  / 0 failed / 0 ignored

`util::fs::tests` is 14 and not 15 because one of the three tests this batch adds there,
`a_plain_push_would_mix_separators`, is `#[cfg(windows)]`: it asserts the thing a Windows
path join does, so there is nothing for it to check here. It is compiled by CI and carries a
row in `scripts/ci/platform-gated-tests.tsv` (runs-on `windows`, assumption `drive-path`) rather
than pretending to be covered by the Linux number.

Seventeen tests are new in this batch (5 startup trace, 1 wrong-framing
end-to-end, 2 interpreter resolution, 1 watched files, 3 announced-path,
5 persistent shell), one existing test gained an assertion, one fixture is new, and
one test was rewritten.

== 10. What is still not established

There is no Windows machine here. The claim of this batch is narrower than "the
Windows leg is green": for each of the 42 red tests the mechanism is identified, the
code that produced it is changed, and a test that runs on Linux now exercises that
mechanism. Whether the Windows leg is actually green is decided by the run this push
triggers, and `#[cfg(windows)]`-gated code in these files is still compiled only by
CI. The `local_terminal` item in section 6 is explicitly a test-budget change, not a
finding about Windows shell startup.
